Teeth help us chew food, move our mouths, speak clearly, and even make sounds. Brushing helps keep our gums healthy by removing plaque that can cause tooth decay. It's like giving your toothbrush a gentle bath to remove any harmful particles. Brushing is also important for oral health because it prevents cavities from forming.

How does brushing work? First, you place the bristles of your toothbrush on the back of your tongue and use circular motions with each stroke to clean between your teeth. This makes it easier to reach and remove plaque without damaging the gums or enamel.

Why do we brush? To remove food particles from the teeth, wash them with water and mild soap, then dry them thoroughly with a soft-bristled toothbrush. Patience is key;

